WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.262
Welcome back.

00:00:01.262 --> 00:00:03.259
Let's go beyond a quiz and use

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:05.988
Polling Pops to notice confusion, support

00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:07.719
the room, and check again.

00:00:07.719 --> 00:00:10.657
Start with an experience that suits the
topic.

00:00:10.657 --> 00:00:13.504
The category recommends a preset, and you

00:00:13.504 --> 00:00:15.309
can choose one explicitly.

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:18.171
Campus fits education; Blueprint suits

00:00:18.171 --> 00:00:21.636
technical material; Pops keeps things
playful.

00:00:21.636 --> 00:00:25.876
These change the presentation, not scoring
or timers.

00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:28.397
The host can override the experience for

00:00:28.397 --> 00:00:32.873
one session, and participants keep their
own accessibility preferences.

00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:37.051
In Diagnose, choose whether confidence is

00:00:37.051 --> 00:00:39.175
off, optional, or required.

00:00:39.175 --> 00:00:41.701
Required confidence helps distinguish a

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:44.098
guess from a confident misconception.

00:00:44.098 --> 00:00:46.184
Add a concept key so the report

00:00:46.184 --> 00:00:47.732
can group the evidence.

00:00:47.732 --> 00:00:49.986
You can also label a misleading answer

00:00:49.986 --> 00:00:52.358
and explain why someone might choose it.

00:00:52.358 --> 00:00:54.677
Those notes are for the facilitator.

00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:56.780
They aren't shown to participants while

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:57.912
the question is open.

00:00:57.912 --> 00:01:03.041
Keep the labels specific, useful, and free
of personal judgments.

00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:07.517
Next, use Recover to add a linked recheck.

00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:11.022
Ask about the same concept in a
fresh situation.

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:13.445
Here, we change twenty-four of thirty

00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:15.081
checks to forty of fifty.

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:17.482
The correct method stays the same, but

00:01:17.482 --> 00:01:19.630
the learner has to apply it again.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.568
Mark the answer and add an explanation.

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:25.039
A linked recheck gives stronger session

00:01:25.039 --> 00:01:28.586
evidence than simply asking the original
question twice.

00:01:28.586 --> 00:01:31.454
Preview both questions, wait for Saved,

00:01:31.454 --> 00:01:33.586
and publish the reviewed set.

00:01:34.495 --> 00:01:38.426
If you need context before the questions,
create a Presentation.

00:01:38.426 --> 00:01:41.613
Content slides and interactive questions

00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:43.285
live in one sequence.

00:01:43.285 --> 00:01:45.466
Here we're adding a short briefing and

00:01:45.466 --> 00:01:48.220
an audience question, then checking the
preview.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.733
This is a responsive facilitation tool,

00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:54.792
not a pixel-perfect replacement for a
slide-design application.

00:01:54.792 --> 00:01:56.885
Use a Round for a focused check;

00:01:56.885 --> 00:01:59.502
use a Presentation when explanations and

00:01:59.502 --> 00:02:01.725
questions need to travel together.

00:02:02.662 --> 00:02:05.767
In a live lobby or question, participants

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:08.190
can say Got it, I'm unsure, Show

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.008
an example, or Too fast.

00:02:10.008 --> 00:02:11.929
Let's request an example.

00:02:11.929 --> 00:02:14.297
The Audience panel lets the facilitator

00:02:14.297 --> 00:02:16.787
see the session alias and current signal.

00:02:16.787 --> 00:02:18.970
Public views show totals only, and

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.537
distributions stay hidden until at least

00:02:21.537 --> 00:02:23.784
five people signal in that context.

00:02:23.784 --> 00:02:27.947
Signals don't change scores or
automatically diagnose anyone.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.516
They're an invitation to pause, check

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.251
access, or offer support.

00:02:33.174 --> 00:02:34.567
Chat starts off.

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:36.829
Enable it when a room conversation will

00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:40.250
help, and keep the slow mode appropriate
for your audience.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.729
A participant can send a short message;

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.843
the facilitator can pin it, and people
can react.

00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:48.012
Q and A is separate: it keeps

00:02:48.012 --> 00:02:51.229
questions and facilitator replies
together.

00:02:51.229 --> 00:02:54.184
With education premoderation, the question

00:02:54.184 --> 00:02:57.209
waits for review before appearing
publicly.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:59.837
Publish and answer it when appropriate.

00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:01.935
Moderation controls help you remove

00:03:01.935 --> 00:03:05.172
messages or restrict someone who disrupts
the session.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.315
Now start the diagnostic question.

00:03:08.315 --> 00:03:11.074
In this synthetic group, all five people

00:03:11.074 --> 00:03:14.178
choose the wrong answer with high
confidence.

00:03:14.178 --> 00:03:16.665
Lock and reveal the responses.

00:03:16.665 --> 00:03:19.007
The insight explains the measurement

00:03:19.007 --> 00:03:23.237
behind its suggestion; it is guidance, not
an automated judgment.

00:03:23.237 --> 00:03:25.577
Choose an explanation, work through the

00:03:25.577 --> 00:03:28.637
method with the group, and finish the
intervention.

00:03:28.637 --> 00:03:30.776
Then open the linked recheck.

00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:33.586
Four of our five demo participants now

00:03:33.586 --> 00:03:35.583
apply the method correctly.

00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:38.083
Complete the branch before moving on.

00:03:39.022 --> 00:03:41.727
The report connects the first responses,

00:03:41.727 --> 00:03:44.500
the intervention, and the linked recheck.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.003
Here, four of five initially incorrect

00:03:47.003 --> 00:03:49.835
participants recovered on the new
question.

00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:54.165
Read the numerator and denominator, not
just the percentage.

00:03:54.165 --> 00:03:56.323
It's a small sample, and it shows

00:03:56.323 --> 00:04:00.376
recovery during this session, not proof of
long-term learning.

00:04:00.376 --> 00:04:02.547
Review the unresolved concept and

00:04:02.547 --> 00:04:04.916
interaction summary, and use them to

00:04:04.916 --> 00:04:08.469
decide what deserves another explanation
or follow-up.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.831
For another opportunity to practise, open

00:04:11.831 --> 00:04:14.332
the Round's assignment settings and create

00:04:14.332 --> 00:04:16.118
an account-free practice link.

00:04:16.118 --> 00:04:18.593
Set the access and completion options for

00:04:18.593 --> 00:04:20.525
your audience before sharing it.

00:04:20.525 --> 00:04:22.766
Participants work through the questions

00:04:22.766 --> 00:04:24.778
without becoming workspace members.

00:04:24.778 --> 00:04:27.755
Later, return through Library, Sessions,

00:04:27.755 --> 00:04:31.775
Assignments, or Results to manage what
you've created.

00:04:31.775 --> 00:04:34.945
Features depend on your deployment and
permissions.

00:04:34.945 --> 00:04:37.227
Start with one concept, try the full

00:04:37.227 --> 00:04:39.637
recovery loop, and use the evidence to

00:04:39.637 --> 00:04:41.285
improve your next session.
